WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:06.580
With a story that runs across five hours of
television in December 2019 and January 2020,

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:11.460
Crisis on Infinite Earths is set to include
not just all of the current DC shows, but

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:15.040
also a roster of heroes drawn from a long
history of television.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:19.260
It's exciting stuff, but it raises a question:
just how did we get here?

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:24.579
The CW's version of Crisis on Infinite Earths
is notable for being the first event of its

00:00:24.579 --> 00:00:28.869
kind to take place on television, but the
comic book history that leads up to it starts

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:34.400
all the way back in 1961, or, depending on
how you look at it, maybe even before then.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.340
Back in the '40s, after Superman was introduced
to the public and proved to be wildly popular

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:44.920
and extremely profitable for its publishers,
he inspired a whole wave of brand-new superhero

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:45.420
comics.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.680
Eventually, the company that would become
DC Comics decided to put a few of them in

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:54.420
the same book, creating the Justice Society
of America and the first shared universe in

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:54.920
comics.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:59.760
By the late '40s and early '50s, however,
superheroes fell out of popularity.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.339
Only the most consistently successful heroes
stuck around, while the rest, like the original

00:01:04.339 --> 00:01:09.010
Golden Age versions of the Flash and Green
Lantern, were canceled and seemingly condemned

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:09.920
to obscurity.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:15.520
But then, a decade later, DC decided to dust
off a few of those pulpy concepts and reboot

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.680
them in a slightly more sci-fi direction,
kicking off the Silver Age of Comics with

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.280
an all-new, mostly different version of the
Flash.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:25.480
Right from the start, though, there was something
weird: in that first story, Barry Allen was

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:30.520
inspired to take the name "The Flash" because
he'd read comics about Jay Garrick, the Golden

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:31.380
Age Flash.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.080
With that, DC began to flirt with the idea
of a multiverse, where different versions

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.720
of their characters existed in different realities.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:43.860
This all came together in 1961's famous "The
Flash of Two Worlds," where Barry and Jay

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.420
met up for a single adventure.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:47.900
It turned out that this was just the beginning.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:55.431
By 1964, DC had a multiverse centered on Earth-One,
the somewhat arrogantly named primary DC universe

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.700
that featured the ongoing adventures of the
present-day heroes.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:03.030
Meanwhile, the Golden Age versions politely
allowed themselves to be called Earth-Two

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.960
despite the fact that they came first.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.400
Clearly, there was only one thing left to
do: a crossover!

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:14.660
Justice League of America #21 and 22 featured
"Crisis on Earth-One" and "Crisis on Earth-Two,"

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:18.120
in which the Justice League teamed up with
their Golden Age counterparts in the Justice

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.940
Society to battle for the fate of two universes.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.840
This kicked off two important DC traditions.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:29.820
First was the idea of an annual JLA/JSA crossover,
which continued for the next 20 years.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:34.620
The second was that almost all of these crossovers,
which spread out throughout the DC Multiverse,

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:38.810
were called a "Crisis," making that the shorthand
word for a threat that went beyond the scale

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:40.120
of a single universe.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.640
Eventually, though, things spiraled to the
point of being as big as they possibly could.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.120
In 1985, DC made a very bold promise for their
new series.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.100
"Worlds will live.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.700
Worlds will die.

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.080
And the universe will never be the same."

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.800
For the most part, they delivered with a year-long
event called Crisis on Infinite Earths.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:08.420
The company was restructuring their entire
multiverse into a single streamlined whole,

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:12.280
but rather than just rebooting all the comics,
they made it part of a story.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:18.320
A villain called the Anti-Monitor (who presumably
did not monitor things) was wiping out universes,

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.900
and the ones that remained coalesced into
a single unified timeline.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:26.460
In the process, major characters like the
Flash and Supergirl were killed off with as

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:30.770
much permanence as there can be in superhero
comics, which is to say, they were dead for

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:33.520
about 20 years before they inevitably came
back.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.160
That wasn't quite the end, though.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:40.380
The "Crisis" brand was so strong that DC kept
it around even after they bailed on the multiverse.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:46.160
Another event devoted to fixing up the complicated
DCU timeline was called Zero Hour: Crisis

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.420
in Time, and there was a modern-day JLA/JSA
team-up against the fifth dimension called

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:51.780
"Crisis Times Five."

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.260
In 2006, Infinite Crisis, as its name implies,
came along to reverse all the changes and

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:58.600
reintroduce the multiverse.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.120
The branding loomed large in the minds of
fans as the biggest worlds-shattering event

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:04.560
in DC's long history.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:09.730
One of the things that makes the TV version
of Crisis on Infinite Earths so interesting

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:13.300
is that it's following in the same tradition
as its comic book counterpart.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.019
Once Arrow's surprising success led to a series
of spinoffs and other shows debuted in their

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:23.070
own universes trying to cash in on the massive
popularity of superheroes these days, seeing

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.140
them team up became inevitable.

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:29.600
Much like it had in the comics, the idea of
an annual crossover quickly became a tradition

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:30.800
for the Arrowverse.

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:34.689
It started with Ollie Queen teaming up with
the Flash for a couple of story arcs that

00:04:34.689 --> 00:04:37.050
ran through their respective shows in 2014…

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:38.700
"I get it, Barry, I get it.

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:39.380
You're fast."

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:45.240
….and then a backdoor pilot for Legends
of Tomorrow the following year.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.960
Things really started to pick up in 2016.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.340
That March saw the Supergirl episode "Worlds
Finest," which saw the Flash vibrating his

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:55.520
way to Supergirl's universe, now called Earth-38.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:01.420
That then led to a full-on three-part Arrowverse/Supergirl
crossover called "Invasion!" and then, just

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:06.509
to cap things off, March 2017 saw "Duet,"
an episode of Flash where Supergirl showed

00:05:06.509 --> 00:05:10.889
up for a singing, dancing team-up that took
advantage of the stars' musical theater backgrounds

00:05:10.889 --> 00:05:12.260
with a few catchy tunes.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:13.180
"We're not singing for you!"

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:13.680
"No!"

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:14.940
"...anymore…"

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:15.500
"Anymore."

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:16.000
"...I mean."

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.420
It's worth noting that both "Worlds Finest"
and "Invasion" were both named after crossover

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:26.160
comics: the long-running Superman/Batman team-up
title and a 1988 event in which three alien

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.880
races invaded Earth at the same time, respectively.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.259
That tradition was continued with "Crisis
on Earth-X," which, like the comic it was

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:38.080
named for, featured the introduction of a
world ruled by Nazis, complete with an evil

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:38.580
Supergirl.

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:43.040
It turned out that Earth-X was a secret fifty-third
Earth that nobody ever talked about because

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:45.000
they all agreed it was terrible.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:50.699
In December of 2018, the Arrowverse launched
"Elseworlds," a crossover that ran through

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:55.280
Green Arrow, Flash, and Supergirl, and to
call it ambitious is underselling things quite

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:55.780
a bit.

00:05:55.940 --> 00:06:00.240
For one thing, it involved twisting the setup
of the previous crossovers by also throwing

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:04.689
in a body-swap between the Flash and Arrow,
which gave us a truly delightful moment of

00:06:04.689 --> 00:06:08.000
Grant Gustin growling Oliver Queen's catchphrase
at a robot.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:12.860
"AMAZO, YOU HAVE FAILED THIS CITY."

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:17.600
More importantly, "Elseworlds" served as the
Arrowverse's introduction to Batwoman.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:22.520
In the comics, the modern Batwoman, Kate Kane,
had been introduced in 2006 before taking

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.240
over Detective Comics for a year.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:29.440
As a lesbian, she was DC's most high-profile
queer character, with a backstory that saw

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:33.040
her being dismissed from the Army while at
West Point under the "Don't Ask, Don't Tell"

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:33.540
policy.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.300
She's also Bruce Wayne's cousin, and on the
show, Batman's absence from Gotham City has

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:41.140
inspired her to take on a heroic identity
of her own.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:45.300
Her appearance in the "Elseworlds" crossover,
where she was portrayed by Ruby Rose, paved

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:47.900
the way for Batwoman to join the CW's lineup.

00:06:48.129 --> 00:06:53.420
The thing is, "Elseworlds" was not the self-contained
crossover that "Crisis on Earth-X" was.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:57.649
The specific body swap story was wrapped up,
but the crossover also spent a lot of time

00:06:57.649 --> 00:07:02.289
with the Monitor, the Anti-Monitor, and the
Psycho Pirate, all of whom were key characters

00:07:02.289 --> 00:07:06.389
in the comic book version of Crisis on Infinite
Earths, setting up the next big crossover

00:07:06.389 --> 00:07:07.680
a year in advance.

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:12.759
They even have the other major character introduced
in the original Crisis, Harbinger, who was

00:07:12.759 --> 00:07:17.610
actually brought to TV way back in Arrow's
first season as Lyla Michaels, head of Argus

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:19.080
and wife of John Diggle.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:24.060
Right now, the CW shows have a huge amount
of characters and parallel worlds to play

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.280
with when they're setting up a gigantic multiversal
crossover.

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:31.399
There's the Arrowverse, of course, anchored
by Green Arrow and the Flash, with heroes

00:07:31.399 --> 00:07:36.339
like Vibe, Mr. Terrific, Black Canary, Killer
Frost, and even more obscure characters like

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:39.860
gun-toting weirdo Wild Dog showing up in their
supporting casts.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:44.849
There's also Legends of Tomorrow, with Firestorm,
the Atom, White Canary, Vixen, and more.

00:07:44.849 --> 00:07:49.599
Plus, they folded in John Constantine, bringing
actor Matt Ryan back for the role after the

00:07:49.599 --> 00:07:53.900
cancellation of Constantine, and brought in
Western hero Jonah Hex in an episode where

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:56.039
a twelve foot-tall Care Bear fought a demon.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:57.900
And, of course, we have Batwoman.

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:02.800
Outside of that specific universe, though,
the CW also has Supergirl and the heroes of

00:08:02.809 --> 00:08:08.610
Earth-38, including Kara Danvers, J'onn J'onnz
(the Martian Manhunter), the Guardian (the

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:13.530
masked heroic identity of Superman's Pal Jimmy
Olsen), the Legion of Super-Heroes (a team

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:18.420
from the 31st century with a sprawling roster
all its own) and, you know, Superman.

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:22.260
And the network also airs Black Lightning,
which features the title character, along

00:08:22.271 --> 00:08:26.550
with his daughters Jennifer and Anissa, the
heroines known as Thunder and Lightning, and

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:30.460
Grace Choi, who was a member of the Outsiders
alongside Thunder in the comics.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:31.160
But wait!

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:32.000
There's more!

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:34.240
Those are just the shows going on now.

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:39.599
Before the current run of successful shows,
the CW and its predecessors also ran Smallville,

00:08:39.599 --> 00:08:44.180
which started as a teen drama focused on a
younger Clark Kent but ran for ten years and

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:47.069
wound up including everyone from Hawkman to
Zatanna.

00:08:47.069 --> 00:08:52.220
In 2002, there was the short-lived Birds of
Prey, another take on a female-focused, Batman-free

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:57.480
Gotham City about Batman and Catwoman's daughter,
the Huntress, plus Oracle, and Black Canary.

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:02.280
Those are all in play for TV's Crisis on Infinite
Earths...and that's just the heroes.

00:09:03.509 --> 00:09:07.550
The stage is set for Crisis on Infinite Earths
to hit the airwaves in December, and from

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:10.180
everything we know so far, it's going to be
big.

00:09:10.649 --> 00:09:15.820
"One where the Nazis won World War II, one
where Kennedy was never assassinated…"

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:17.980
"Wait, one where all of us are evil?"

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:19.920
"Been there, it sucks."

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:24.460
We already know that characters from Smallville
are going to be showing up, with Tom Welling

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:28.920
and Erica Durance reprising their roles as
that universe's Clark Kent and Lois Lane.

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:33.460
Perhaps surprisingly, Birds of Prey is back
in the mix, too, with Ashley Scott stepping

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:38.580
back in the role of Helena Kyle, the Huntress,
17 years after Birds of Prey was canceled.

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:40.780
There's even more stunt casting, too.

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:45.580
Kevin Conroy, who has been the voice of Batman
in various video games and animated projects

00:09:45.589 --> 00:09:50.269
since he took on the career-defining role
for Batman: The Animated Series in 1992, will

00:09:50.269 --> 00:09:53.400
be playing a live-action Bruce Wayne for the
first time.

00:09:53.509 --> 00:09:57.380
The details have yet to be released by the
CW, but he definitely looks like he'll be

00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:00.560
playing the older version of Bruce that he
voiced on Batman Beyond.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.020
Even more intriguing?

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:08.080
Burt Ward, who played Robin on the 1966 Batman
television show alongside the late Adam West,

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.340
has also been cast in an unannounced role.

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:15.220
Also, if Tyler Hoechlin and Tom Welling weren't
enough Supermen for you, Crisis on Infinite

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:18.649
Earths also marks Brandon Routh's return to
playing the Man of Steel.

00:10:18.649 --> 00:10:23.389
The weird thing here is that while he starred
in 2006's Superman Returns, Routh already

00:10:23.389 --> 00:10:27.100
has a role in the Arrowverse as Ray Palmer,
better known as the Atom.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:31.900
Seeing him get another shot as an older Superman
with a costume inspired by Mark Waid and Alex

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:34.520
Ross's Kingdom Come storyline is intriguing.

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:38.660
It's not just that Routh will presumably be
pulling double duty, but because whatever

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:43.480
the big problem is in Crisis, it's going to
need at least three Supermen to solve it.

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:49.120
Even more than the destruction of the DC multiverse,
the Crisis on Infinite Earths comic book is

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:52.160
remembered for featuring the deaths of Supergirl
and the Flash.

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:56.520
That might seem pretty ominous, considering
that the TV version is going to prominently

00:10:56.529 --> 00:11:01.070
feature both of those characters, but in reality,
they're probably the two major characters

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:02.660
who have the least to worry about.

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:07.340
They are, after all, the stars of their respective
shows, which from all indications are both

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:09.460
going strong into the indefinite future.

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:14.030
With the CW announcing that Arrow's eighth
season will be its last, Oliver Queen, on

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:16.180
the other hand, might not be so safe.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:18.200
Even there, though, it seems unlikely.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:22.320
There are still two more episodes of Arrow
scheduled to air after Crisis on Infinite

00:11:22.329 --> 00:11:26.040
Earths ends, and while one wrap-up episode
about Ollie's pals dealing with his death

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.660
would be understandable, two would be pushing
it.

00:11:28.780 --> 00:11:32.300
That doesn't mean they won't pull it off,
though, stranger things have happened.

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:36.680
Once you've done an interdimensional team-up
musical, nothing's out of the question.

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:38.900
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:43.360
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
DC storylines are coming soon.

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:47.160
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

